    "c27": {
      "anchor": "rs-invitations#markdown-1",
      "kind": "markdown",
      "label": "7.3 Invitations and the letter",
      "excerpt": "Invitations aren't a permission: they're a rule that counts actual invitations r",
      "text": "No.  **Managing tickets is part of the app.**",
      "by": "owenoak",
      "at": "2026-11-02T03:36:52.717Z",
      "status": "answered",
      "quote": "In the first version, the write server only checks that tickets are left. How many one person may request is checked by the page in their browser",
      "offset": 376,
      "edited": "2026-11-02T03:37:10.653Z",
      "replies": [
        {
          "by": "owenoak",
          "agent": "claude",
          "at": "2026-11-02T03:38:43.539Z",
          "html": "<p>Done:  \"<b>Managing tickets is part of the app.</b>  It counts what's left and what each person may request, and the write server only checks who may write.\"</p>\n<p>The app still has to handle two people asking for the last ticket, so I added one general rule:  a write made from an out-of-date copy is refused, and the second person is told it's gone.  Whether a host can do that inside an ATSpace hasn't been tested.</p>"
        }
      ],
      "claim": {
        "by": "owenoak",
        "agent": "claude",
        "at": "2026-11-02T03:38:28.899Z"
      }
    },
